WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.690
What do you say now?

2
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.961
I don't know.

3
00:00:21.036 --> 00:00:23.799
You said your dad
wouldn't be home for an hour.

4
00:00:23.872 --> 00:00:26.772
I know, but it-You don't know-
Don't you love me?

5
00:00:26.842 --> 00:00:29.571
Of course. You know l-
Then, come on.
Let's do it.

6
00:00:30.645 --> 00:00:32.635
All right.

7
00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:47.054
Where are they?
Nightstand.

8
00:00:53.165 --> 00:00:56.031
Gentlemen, start your engines.

9
00:00:56.102 --> 00:00:58.092
[Engine Revs]

10
00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:03.801
[Both Laughing]
I so want this car!

11
00:01:03.876 --> 00:01:05.934
Whoo-hoo!

12
00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:08.410
Whoo-hoo!
[Laughing Continues]

13
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.004
- Whoo-hoo.!
- [Coughing]

14
00:01:15.487 --> 00:01:18.580
[Coughing Continues]

15
00:01:20.724 --> 00:01:22.953
[Horn Honks]

16
00:01:23.027 --> 00:01:25.688
[Coughing Continues]

17
00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:30.498
Oh, my God!
You're bleeding.

18
00:01:30.566 --> 00:01:32.863
Look out!

19
00:01:32.935 --> 00:01:35.528
- [Truck Horn Blaring]
- [Screaming]

20
00:01:42.177 --> 00:01:44.667
- [Horn Blaring]
- [Screaming]

21
00:01:44.746 --> 00:01:46.144
[Crash]

22
00:02:32.623 --> 00:02:34.556
What lie are they telling you?

23
00:02:34.625 --> 00:02:38.685
I'll check on it.
Okay. Yes-

24
00:02:38.762 --> 00:02:42.197
- [Tapping On Counter]
- Come on.

25
00:02:42.265 --> 00:02:45.529
- Okay, thank you.
- [Phone Receiver Hangs Up]

26
00:02:45.602 --> 00:02:47.899
Okay, pharmaceuticals
were delivered this morning.

27
00:02:47.971 --> 00:02:50.905
But Shipping accidentally sent
the box with Vicodin to Research.

28
00:02:50.973 --> 00:02:53.406
Hmm. That's a tough one.

29
00:02:53.476 --> 00:02:57.536
If only we had some way
to communicate with
another part of the building.

30
00:02:58.948 --> 00:03:00.881
Sixteen-year-old
M.V.A. Victim.

31
00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.248
He's been in and out of the hospital
for three weeks with internal bleeding.
No one can find the cause.

32
00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:07.219
Internal bleeding after a car accident.
Wow, that's shocking.

33
00:03:07.288 --> 00:03:09.619
Let me talk to Shipping.
I speak their language.

34
00:03:09.691 --> 00:03:13.285
It's been three weeks-
Your hospital doesn't have
my pain medication.

35
00:03:13.361 --> 00:03:15.851
Shipping says
it's going to be an hour.

36
00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.862
This is Dr. Cuddy.
What's going on?

37
00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:20.865
The crash didn't cause the bleed.
Right- the bleed
caused the crash.

38
00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:24.528
Blood got on the road, got all slippery-
Anyone here got drugs?

39
00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:30.302
She saw his blood,
she got distracted,
and she crashed his dad's Porsche.

40
00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.967
- Dad'll love that.
- He was-

41
00:03:32.044 --> 00:03:33.601
- Don't talk.
- It's gonna be an hour.

42
00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.805
Thank God you took control.

43
00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.816
- If you can't wait one hour to get-
- Kid's got hemolytic anemia.

44
00:03:38.884 --> 00:03:40.748
Kid? How old?

45
00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:44.219
He must have inherited it.
He's gonna die. My condolences.

46
00:03:44.289 --> 00:03:47.052
Wasn't inherited. The problem's
outside the red blood cells.

47
00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:49.786
This is impossible.
A 16-year-old does not
get hemolytic anemia.

48
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.989
Give her back the file.
You have bigger problems
to tend to, like my meds.

49
00:03:54.064 --> 00:03:57.362
Elevated indirect bilirubin,
low serum haptoglobin-

50
00:03:57.435 --> 00:03:59.959
- He's got meningitis.
- Uh, no.

51
00:04:02.505 --> 00:04:04.597
- Artificial heart valve.
- No.

52
00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.670
Get everyone in my office.

53
00:04:13.682 --> 00:04:16.241
Kid's gonna be dead
in a matter of days...

54
00:04:16.318 --> 00:04:19.252
if we don't figure out why
his red blood cells are disintegrating.

55
00:04:19.321 --> 00:04:21.788
So, differential diagnosis, people.
Well,
it's not environmental.

56
00:04:21.857 --> 00:04:25.087
Dad hired a company to clean the house,
maid washed all the clothes...

57
00:04:25.159 --> 00:04:27.092
and bought hypoallergenic
sheets and pillows.

58
00:04:27.161 --> 00:04:29.094
- You want us to recheck?
- No.

59
00:04:29.163 --> 00:04:31.130
If it's environmental,
he'll get better just from staying here.

60
00:04:31.199 --> 00:04:33.632
- It could be an infection.
- No fever. No white count.

61
00:04:33.701 --> 00:04:36.362
- Well, he's 99.2.
- Barely above normal.

62
00:04:36.437 --> 00:04:39.428
But above.
His body's reacting to something.

63
00:04:39.506 --> 00:04:42.440
[Cameron] We could account for the lack
of fever and white count if it's lupus.

64
00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:46.137
[Chase] Drugs will fit just as much as lupus.
Meth will cause hemolytic anemia.

65
00:04:46.213 --> 00:04:48.146
[Cameron]
A lot of meth.

66
00:04:48.215 --> 00:04:50.273
[Foreman]
Also doesn't exactly
seem like the type.

67
00:04:50.349 --> 00:04:54.375
Because his dad drives a Porsche?
Rich kids do drugs just like poor kids.

68
00:04:54.453 --> 00:04:56.386
Didn't mean to offend you.
Okay.!

69
00:04:56.455 --> 00:04:59.321
So it's infection, lupus,
drugs or cancer.

70
00:04:59.391 --> 00:05:02.791
- Cancer?
- Why not? Great meeting.

71
00:05:02.861 --> 00:05:06.057
Shouldn't we narrow it down
before we finish?

72
00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.154
My leg gave us till 11:15.

73
00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:10.564
I'll talk to Wilson about lymphoma,
A.N.A. For lupus,

74
00:05:10.635 --> 00:05:12.966
radioimmunoassay for drugs,

75
00:05:13.036 --> 00:05:15.970
and you test for
whatever you thought it was.

76
00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:18.506
I've got a date
with the pharmacist.

77
00:05:18.575 --> 00:05:20.906
That's already done, and-

78
00:05:20.978 --> 00:05:24.344
[Sighs] Come on!
Come on, come on, come on.

79
00:05:24.413 --> 00:05:26.403
Yeah.

80
00:05:35.024 --> 00:05:37.014
Excuse me.

81
00:05:40.395 --> 00:05:42.521
You know, there are other ways
to manage pain.

82
00:05:42.597 --> 00:05:44.325
Like what? Laughter?

83
00:05:44.399 --> 00:05:47.196
Meditation? Got a guy
that can fix my third chakra?

84
00:05:47.269 --> 00:05:49.361
You're addicted.

85
00:05:49.436 --> 00:05:53.872
If the pills ran my life, I'd agree with you,
but it's my leg that's busy
calendaring what I can't do.

86
00:05:53.941 --> 00:05:55.874
You're in denial.

87
00:05:55.943 --> 00:06:00.276
Right. I never had an infarction in my leg.
No dead muscle, no nerve damage.

88
00:06:00.346 --> 00:06:02.779
Doesn't even hurt.
Actually, it kinda tickles.

89
00:06:02.849 --> 00:06:04.782
And chicks dig this...

90
00:06:04.851 --> 00:06:06.784
better than a puppy.

91
00:06:06.853 --> 00:06:09.787
It's not just your leg.
You wanna get high.

92
00:06:09.856 --> 00:06:11.789
You're doing, what,
80 milligrams a day?

93
00:06:11.857 --> 00:06:13.790
No. That's way too much.

94
00:06:13.859 --> 00:06:16.793
Moderation is the key.
Unless there's pain.

95
00:06:16.862 --> 00:06:19.295
It's double what you were
taking when I hired you.

96
00:06:19.364 --> 00:06:21.228
'Cause you're twice as annoying.

97
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.233
I can't always be here
to protect you.

98
00:06:23.301 --> 00:06:25.234
Patients talk.
Doctors talk.

99
00:06:25.303 --> 00:06:28.738
About how big
your ass has gotten lately?
Not me. I defend it.

100
00:06:28.806 --> 00:06:30.796
You got back.

101
00:06:32.109 --> 00:06:34.099
[Bell Dings]

102
00:06:38.582 --> 00:06:42.244
You can't go a week
without the drugs.
No, I don't wanna go without the drugs.

103
00:06:42.319 --> 00:06:44.252
It'll hurt.
No, you can't.

104
00:06:44.321 --> 00:06:47.255
If you're just getting off
pain medication, it will hurt.

105
00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:50.257
You won't be having a great time,
but you'll make it.

106
00:06:50.326 --> 00:06:52.759
If you're detoxing,
you'll have chills, nausea.

107
00:06:52.828 --> 00:06:55.421
Your pain
will magnify five, ten times.

108
00:06:55.497 --> 00:06:58.431
You won't make it.
Well, I guess we'll never know.

109
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.434
I'll give you a week off clinic duty
if you can go a week without narcotics.

110
00:07:01.502 --> 00:07:03.492
No way.
I love the clinic.

111
00:07:03.571 --> 00:07:06.164
You love the pills.
Two weeks.

112
00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.072
The pills don't make me high.
They make me neutral.
A month.

113
00:07:14.081 --> 00:07:16.071
You're on, mister.

114
00:07:20.187 --> 00:07:24.350
Drugs could cause this?
Cocaine and meth are very hard
on the blood system.

115
00:07:25.358 --> 00:07:28.349
Has he had
any erratic behavior?
No, but-

116
00:07:30.896 --> 00:07:32.954
She was in rehab in
the ninth grade.

117
00:07:33.032 --> 00:07:36.125
She's supposedly
clean now, but-
She obviously cares for him.

118
00:07:36.201 --> 00:07:38.259
Yeah, what she cared about
was the car.

119
00:07:38.336 --> 00:07:40.860
Anniversary present for my wife.

120
00:07:40.939 --> 00:07:44.931
We drove it up north
to watch the leaves change.

121
00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:46.943
She was dead a year later.

122
00:07:47.011 --> 00:07:49.478
Cancer.
I'm sorry.

123
00:07:51.215 --> 00:07:54.775
Mr. Foster,
we're gonna test Keith for drugs.

124
00:07:54.852 --> 00:07:56.785
L- I don't do drugs.

125
00:07:56.854 --> 00:07:59.583
It's not that
we don't trust you, but-

126
00:08:00.723 --> 00:08:04.988
[Cameron]
His hair will tell us any drugs
he's taken over the past 60 days.

127
00:08:05.061 --> 00:08:08.052
It's kind oflike
rings on a tree.

128
00:08:14.636 --> 00:08:16.626
Negative.

129
00:08:17.973 --> 00:08:20.338
- Have you been sick?
- No, nothing.

130
00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:25.038
- Have you been out of the country?
- We went to China,
but we got all our shots before we left.

131
00:08:25.112 --> 00:08:27.045
[Cameron]
It could be an infection.

132
00:08:27.114 --> 00:08:29.604
We're going to give him
a gallium scan just to be safe.

133
00:08:29.684 --> 00:08:32.652
We inject a radioactive isotope
into his bloodstream,

134
00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.745
and we check to see if there's
inflammation anywhere in the body.

135
00:08:35.822 --> 00:08:38.221
Has he ever complained
of anyjoint pain?

136
00:08:38.291 --> 00:08:41.157
Sensitivity to light?
Rashes?

137
00:08:41.227 --> 00:08:44.525
- No, no. Nothing.
- Any relatives who've ever
been diagnosed with lupus?

138
00:08:44.597 --> 00:08:46.860
I don't even know what that is.

139
00:08:46.932 --> 00:08:49.866
In simple terms,
the body becomes allergic to itself.

140
00:08:49.935 --> 00:08:53.028
The immune system attacks
healthy cells in the circulatory system
and destroys them.

141
00:08:53.105 --> 00:08:55.095
Would it be treatable?

142
00:08:56.275 --> 00:08:58.265
It can be manageable.

143
00:08:59.343 --> 00:09:01.276
We can test for the antibodies.

144
00:09:01.345 --> 00:09:04.939
Ninety-five percent of patients
with lupus have positive A.N.A.

145
00:09:06.017 --> 00:09:08.450
Not cloudy.
Negative.

146
00:09:08.519 --> 00:09:12.955
What about bruising?
Never complained of tenderness
under his arms or his groin?

147
00:09:13.023 --> 00:09:15.684
I'm not sure he'd tell me ifhe did.

148
00:09:15.759 --> 00:09:19.250
I guess I don't really know
what's going on in his life.

149
00:09:19.329 --> 00:09:21.990
- He's a teenager.
- Yeah.

150
00:09:25.968 --> 00:09:28.959
What type of cancer
did your wife have?

151
00:09:29.972 --> 00:09:31.871
Pancreatic.

152
00:09:31.941 --> 00:09:34.966
It's his lymph nodes
we're concerned about.

153
00:09:35.043 --> 00:09:38.478
We're going to do a biopsy
to check for lymphoma.

154
00:09:39.848 --> 00:09:42.145
Can you feel this?
No.

155
00:09:42.217 --> 00:09:44.707
Good.

156
00:09:52.226 --> 00:09:54.159
I have cancer, don't I?

157
00:09:54.228 --> 00:09:56.787
We're just testing.

158
00:09:56.863 --> 00:09:58.796
[Sniffles]

159
00:09:58.865 --> 00:10:01.628
That's what they told my mom.

160
00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:10.867
Definitely not cancer.

161
00:10:11.877 --> 00:10:13.935
- Nothing?
- Nothing.

162
00:10:14.012 --> 00:10:16.673
Negative for drugs.
A.N.A. Was negative.
Gallium scan was clear.

163
00:10:16.748 --> 00:10:18.681
Yeah, I got that from the "nothing."

164
00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.013
- Where's his hematocrit?
- Thirteen.

165
00:10:21.085 --> 00:10:25.418
Drops any lower, he's not gonna
have enough red blood cells
to bring oxygen to his body.

166
00:10:25.489 --> 00:10:27.683
He'll suffocate with his lungs
working perfectly.
You okay?

167
00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.889
Excuse me, someone?
Help. Please!

168
00:10:32.896 --> 00:10:35.455
Polite for a dying kid.

169
00:10:39.035 --> 00:10:41.025
How long has it been?

170
00:10:41.104 --> 00:10:43.037
I'm fine.

171
00:10:43.106 --> 00:10:45.630
- There's something in my eye.
Up at the top.
- Which eye?

172
00:10:45.707 --> 00:10:47.833
This one.
What's happening?

173
00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.036
It's all right.
Just look down for me.

174
00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.541
It's clear.
There's nothing in it.

175
00:10:54.616 --> 00:10:56.640
- It's getting worse.
- Is it fuzzy or-

176
00:10:56.717 --> 00:10:58.741
No, it's dark.

177
00:11:00.121 --> 00:11:02.111
I can't see.

178
00:11:15.468 --> 00:11:17.662
[Foreman]
It's a retinal clot in the left eye.

179
00:11:17.737 --> 00:11:20.136
Coumadin would dissolve the clot,
fix his eyesight.

180
00:11:20.206 --> 00:11:23.140
You can't use blood thinners.
He's got internal bleeding.

181
00:11:23.209 --> 00:11:26.507
Fix the eye,
you'd kill everything else.
Surgery's out for the same reason.

182
00:11:26.579 --> 00:11:30.207
We have two hours to figure this out.
Either we restore the blood flow,
or he loses the eye.

183
00:11:31.683 --> 00:11:37.120
Forget the eye.
Tell him to use the other one
to look on the bright side.

184
00:11:37.188 --> 00:11:39.178
The clot tells us something.

185
00:11:40.258 --> 00:11:43.420
Could help us figure out what he has,
which could mean he gets to live.

186
00:11:43.493 --> 00:11:46.723
Differential diagnosis, people.

187
00:11:46.797 --> 00:11:49.560
How does internal bleeding
suddenly start clotting?

188
00:11:49.633 --> 00:11:52.863
It makes no sense.
They're opposing processes.

189
00:11:52.936 --> 00:11:55.802
It can happen in lupus.
Increased platelet count
can cause blood clots.

190
00:11:55.872 --> 00:11:58.203
A.N.A. Was negative.
It's not lupus.

191
00:11:58.274 --> 00:12:01.572
This is true.
But why are you the one saying it?

192
00:12:01.644 --> 00:12:05.704
What are you doing here?
I thought we'd ruled out cancer.

193
00:12:05.781 --> 00:12:07.714
I was lonely.

194
00:12:07.782 --> 00:12:09.806
Well, go see Cuddy.
She needs a friend.

195
00:12:09.885 --> 00:12:12.216
That's funny.
She said you might need one.

196
00:12:12.287 --> 00:12:16.154
That's why you're here.
She wants you to keep an eye on me.
Make sure I don't cheat.

197
00:12:16.224 --> 00:12:19.750
No, I wanted to make sure you don't
start firing shots from the clock tower.

198
00:12:19.827 --> 00:12:22.260
I'm fine-
[Groans]

199
00:12:22.329 --> 00:12:24.262
What's going on?

200
00:12:24.331 --> 00:12:26.560
He hasn't had Vicodin
in over a day.

201
00:12:27.835 --> 00:12:30.826
- Does your leg hurt?
- You ever been shot?

202
00:12:31.905 --> 00:12:35.271
There's gonna be side effects.
Insomnia, depression, tachycardia.

203
00:12:35.341 --> 00:12:37.433
Withdrawal symptoms.
Not applicable.

204
00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:41.502
The only side effects
I'm gonna have are some pain
and 30 days of freedom.

205
00:12:43.916 --> 00:12:46.941
Am I the only one who's
concerned about a dying kid?

206
00:12:47.019 --> 00:12:49.350
If it's not lupus, what else?

207
00:12:49.421 --> 00:12:53.481
Most likely candidate
for throwing a clot
is infection or cancer.

208
00:12:53.557 --> 00:12:55.649
Checked the biopsy twice.
It's not cancer.

209
00:12:55.726 --> 00:12:58.489
It's not an infection.
Gallium scan didn't reveal anything.

210
00:12:58.562 --> 00:13:01.621
Okay.
What hides from a Gallium scan?

211
00:13:05.568 --> 00:13:08.001
Cardiac.
Right.

212
00:13:08.071 --> 00:13:12.063
Clot flicks off, travels through the arteries,
then gets backed up in the eye.

213
00:13:18.781 --> 00:13:21.510
I'm sorry. I wasn't paying attention.
What happened?

214
00:13:21.583 --> 00:13:24.142
It's an infection in his heart.

215
00:13:24.219 --> 00:13:26.209
Great.

216
00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:31.552
Echocardiogram for the heart
and I.V. Antibiotics for the infection, stat.

217
00:13:45.605 --> 00:13:47.595
Is it my birthday?

218
00:13:49.943 --> 00:13:52.638
I'm not lonely.
My leg hurts.

219
00:13:52.712 --> 00:13:55.942
- She's a real masseuse.
- She's $500 an hour, minimum.

220
00:13:56.015 --> 00:13:59.541
She's hot, so she's a hooker?
What kind of pathetic logic is that?

221
00:13:59.618 --> 00:14:03.348
The envious, jealous,
l-never-got-any-in-high-school
kind of logic. Hello!

222
00:14:03.422 --> 00:14:05.753
She's a legitimate masseuse.

223
00:14:05.824 --> 00:14:07.814
Come on.

224
00:14:08.827 --> 00:14:10.760
God! She's beautiful.

225
00:14:10.829 --> 00:14:14.491
Because she's beautiful, I should do it?
What kind of pathetic logic is that?

226
00:14:14.566 --> 00:14:18.934
The envious, jealous,
I'm-married-and-l-can't-do-anything
logic. Hello.

227
00:14:19.003 --> 00:14:22.165
Hi. Uh, listen,
I'm sure you're really good at
whatever it is you do, but-

228
00:14:22.239 --> 00:14:25.105
Deme su mano.
Huh?

229
00:14:27.011 --> 00:14:29.774
- Hey, no, no. Let go of my hand.
- She doesn't speak English.

230
00:14:29.846 --> 00:14:31.745
Shh.

231
00:14:33.182 --> 00:14:35.012
Ow!

232
00:14:35.084 --> 00:14:37.574
Ow-Ahh.

233
00:14:37.653 --> 00:14:41.053
Ahh.

234
00:14:44.459 --> 00:14:46.449
Oh, my God.

235
00:14:50.932 --> 00:14:52.922
Bueno.

236
00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.433
Take off your clothes.

237
00:14:59.774 --> 00:15:01.934
Not a fan of the Stroganoff?

238
00:15:03.444 --> 00:15:07.539
I'm not hungry.
Well, the antibiotics
can cause nausea.

239
00:15:07.614 --> 00:15:09.604
So can the food.

240
00:15:10.784 --> 00:15:13.718
Shouldn't you
be looking at my eye?

241
00:15:13.787 --> 00:15:15.754
The blood clot
isn't life-threatening.

242
00:15:15.821 --> 00:15:18.550
We're focusing on figuring out
the cause of your problems.

243
00:15:18.624 --> 00:15:22.457
So the- So the blindness
will be permanent, won't it?

244
00:15:40.243 --> 00:15:42.403
Thank you.
Bye.

245
00:15:45.082 --> 00:15:47.015
I had a massage.

246
00:15:47.084 --> 00:15:50.519
Looks like you had a masseuse.
Help the pain?

247
00:15:50.587 --> 00:15:53.020
I'm fine.
I know.

248
00:15:53.089 --> 00:15:56.353
Kid's echo was normal.
No signs of any vegetations
on heart valves.

249
00:15:56.425 --> 00:15:59.188
Never met a diagnostic study
I couldn't refute.

250
00:15:59.261 --> 00:16:01.194
And the antibiotics
aren't doing anything.

251
00:16:01.263 --> 00:16:04.197
So double the dosage.
70 milligrams.

252
00:16:04.265 --> 00:16:06.198
That'll box his kidneys
for sure.

253
00:16:06.267 --> 00:16:08.200
Oh, you're right.
Save the kidney.

254
00:16:08.269 --> 00:16:11.203
The guy we transplant it into
will be grateful.

255
00:16:11.272 --> 00:16:14.206
Also, I have an idea
for his eye.
Nothing we can do about his eye.

256
00:16:14.275 --> 00:16:16.640
He's got a clot in the retina-
Put it in a memo.

257
00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.644
If we remove some of the liquid
from the eye itself,
the vitreous humour,

258
00:16:19.713 --> 00:16:22.704
it might make some extra room
around the retinal artery.

259
00:16:24.218 --> 00:16:28.449
If the artery expands,
the clot might move on its own.
That's very creative.

260
00:16:28.521 --> 00:16:30.954
Why didn't you mention this
before?

261
00:16:31.023 --> 00:16:33.013
I didn't think of it before.

262
00:16:33.092 --> 00:16:35.423
You should've.

263
00:16:43.735 --> 00:16:45.725
[Sighs]

264
00:16:53.978 --> 00:16:55.911
This is going to hurt.

265
00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:59.415
Your eye is numb.
You'll only feel pressure.

266
00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:27.830
Give it a minute.

267
00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:37.883
I can see.

268
00:17:37.951 --> 00:17:39.577
[Laughs]

269
00:18:06.444 --> 00:18:08.377
I can see you.

270
00:18:08.446 --> 00:18:10.879
I heard.
Congratulations.

271
00:18:10.949 --> 00:18:13.974
Don't. I haven't
brushed my teeth in two days.

272
00:18:19.022 --> 00:18:21.455
[Sighs]

273
00:18:21.525 --> 00:18:24.584
I'm so scared
they're not gonna find out
what's wrong with you.

274
00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:27.457
No biggie.
I'm fine.

275
00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:31.590
I feel so bad about all this.
It's all my fault.

276
00:18:31.667 --> 00:18:33.896
No. No, it's not.

277
00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:37.928
But your father- he hates me.

278
00:18:38.006 --> 00:18:40.599
He's just pissed about his car.

279
00:18:45.647 --> 00:18:47.477
[Retches]
[Gasps]

280
00:18:47.549 --> 00:18:50.016
Help! Help! Help!

281
00:18:50.084 --> 00:18:53.075
[Coughing]

282
00:18:59.026 --> 00:19:01.323
What's wrong?

283
00:19:01.394 --> 00:19:03.918
A.S.T. Is 859.
We're getting him to the I.C.U.

284
00:19:03.997 --> 00:19:05.964
A.L.T. And G.G.T. Are in the tank.
Our antibiotics-

285
00:19:06.032 --> 00:19:08.693
[House]
Would not have caused this.
She must have given him drugs!

286
00:19:08.768 --> 00:19:11.600
- I wouldn't do that!
- It's not drugs!

287
00:19:11.671 --> 00:19:14.298
His liver is shutting down.
What?

288
00:19:14.373 --> 00:19:18.035
What does that mean?
It means he's all better.
He's ready to go home.

289
00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:20.600
What?
What do you think it means?

290
00:19:20.679 --> 00:19:23.146
You can't live without a liver.
He's dying.

291
00:19:23.215 --> 00:19:25.842
What is your problem?
Bum leg.
What's yours?

292
00:19:25.916 --> 00:19:28.406
Hey, we don't have time for this.
Let's go.

293
00:19:33.591 --> 00:19:35.888
His son's dying,
and you're mocking him?

294
00:19:35.959 --> 00:19:38.222
It was a dumb question.

295
00:19:38.295 --> 00:19:39.818
No, it wasn't.

296
00:19:42.165 --> 00:19:44.155
You're right. It wasn't.

297
00:19:45.969 --> 00:19:48.334
Is proving Cuddy wrong
worth all this?

298
00:20:04.018 --> 00:20:07.783
- You know, House shouldn't even be here.
- Because he said something inappropriate?

299
00:20:07.855 --> 00:20:11.449
If we sent him home
every time he did that,
we wouldn't need this office.

300
00:20:11.525 --> 00:20:15.893
- He's in pain.
- [Scoffs] What does the man
have to do to piss you off?

301
00:20:15.962 --> 00:20:18.828
- He's been without pain relief
for over 70 hours.
- Exactly!

302
00:20:18.899 --> 00:20:21.389
He's detoxing.
Can't you see
he's out of his mind?

303
00:20:21.468 --> 00:20:23.458
[House]
That's what they said about Manson.

304
00:20:26.105 --> 00:20:28.038
Do you wanna continue
talking about me,

305
00:20:28.107 --> 00:20:30.574
or should we discuss
what the liver damage tells us?

306
00:20:33.212 --> 00:20:35.736
I was born in a log cabin in Illinois-

307
00:20:35.814 --> 00:20:37.747
Hemolytic anemia
doesn't cause liver damage.

308
00:20:37.816 --> 00:20:39.680
Add the fact he's coughing blood,

309
00:20:39.751 --> 00:20:42.378
you've got three of the indicators
of organ-threatening lupus.

310
00:20:42.454 --> 00:20:44.148
It's moving too fast.

311
00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:47.748
Could be hepatitis "E."

312
00:20:47.825 --> 00:20:50.520
There's only been one case of hep "E"
originating in the U.S. Since-

313
00:20:50.594 --> 00:20:54.654
His history says he's been
in and out of the country
four times in the last year.

314
00:20:54.732 --> 00:20:56.494
You really think he's got hep "E"?

315
00:20:56.567 --> 00:20:59.160
No.
I think lupus is way more likely.

316
00:20:59.235 --> 00:21:02.032
All right, then let's start him
on I.V. Cytoxan and plasmapheresis.

317
00:21:02.105 --> 00:21:04.868
No, we should rule out hep "E."

318
00:21:04.941 --> 00:21:06.874
You just said it wasn't hep "E."

319
00:21:06.943 --> 00:21:09.934
I said lupus is way more likely,
but if we treat for lupus
and it is hep "E"-

320
00:21:10.012 --> 00:21:12.707
He's toast.
Exactly.

321
00:21:12.781 --> 00:21:14.714
But there isn't a treatment
for hepatitis "E."

322
00:21:14.783 --> 00:21:17.444
Either he'll get better on his own
or he'll continue to deteriorate.

323
00:21:17.519 --> 00:21:21.045
Yeah. I went to medical school too.

324
00:21:21.123 --> 00:21:23.113
Start him on solumedrol.

325
00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:27.125
- If he's got hep "E,"
that's only gonna make him worse.
- Not as much.

326
00:21:27.194 --> 00:21:29.127
It's Goldilocks, people.

327
00:21:29.196 --> 00:21:33.655
Won't hurt him so much
that it'll kill him, and it won't hurt him
so little that we can't tell.

328
00:21:33.734 --> 00:21:35.928
It'll hurt him... just right.

329
00:21:36.002 --> 00:21:37.935
And if it does nothing-

330
00:21:38.004 --> 00:21:41.461
We'll know it's not hep "E"
and can start treating for lupus.

331
00:21:41.541 --> 00:21:44.065
Now watch me do it
while drinking a glass of water.

332
00:21:44.144 --> 00:21:48.375
What do we tell the dad?
"We think your kid has lupus,
so we're gonna treat him for hepatitis 'E.'

333
00:21:48.447 --> 00:21:52.780
And, oh, yeah, if it really is hep 'E,'
we're not actually giving him
hep 'E ' medication.

334
00:21:52.851 --> 00:21:55.614
So it's gonna make him worse,
not better."
Think he'll go for that?

335
00:21:56.789 --> 00:21:59.154
- So you want us to lie?
- No.

336
00:21:59.223 --> 00:22:02.123
- I want you to lie.
- Why me?

337
00:22:02.193 --> 00:22:04.183
Because he trusts you.

338
00:22:06.831 --> 00:22:09.765
- This is a mistake.
- This is a lawsuit.

339
00:22:09.833 --> 00:22:11.766
Hep "E" is possible.

340
00:22:11.835 --> 00:22:14.268
House always pulls these stunts,
and he's right more often-

341
00:22:14.337 --> 00:22:18.136
He's delaying treatment because
of a one-in-a-million chance that
even he doesn't think is the problem.

342
00:22:18.208 --> 00:22:20.141
I don't wanna lie to him.
Then don't.

343
00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:23.144
And get fired?
Oh, like he's gonna fire you.
He loves you.

344
00:22:23.212 --> 00:22:25.475
[Bell Dings]
I've gotta do something.

345
00:22:25.548 --> 00:22:28.482
- Kid needs treatment.
- Treat him for lupus.

346
00:22:28.551 --> 00:22:30.643
That will get you fired.

347
00:22:31.787 --> 00:22:33.777
You really think House
is losing it?

348
00:22:33.855 --> 00:22:36.015
[Scoffs]
Yeah.

349
00:22:36.958 --> 00:22:38.948
He's fine.
He knows what he's doing.

350
00:23:09.621 --> 00:23:12.612
[Exhaling]

351
00:23:22.399 --> 00:23:24.127
[Yells]

352
00:23:24.201 --> 00:23:26.134
[Sighs]

353
00:23:26.203 --> 00:23:28.432
[Chuckles]

354
00:23:28.506 --> 00:23:31.838
[Murmurs]
Okay. [Exhales]

355
00:23:40.216 --> 00:23:43.480
We're recommending
a drug called solumedrol.

356
00:23:43.553 --> 00:23:48.114
For hepatitis?
Did that show up
on his blood tests?

357
00:23:49.124 --> 00:23:52.559
The tests
are never 100% accurate.

358
00:23:52.628 --> 00:23:55.357
Well, then all the other tests
could be wrong too.

359
00:23:55.430 --> 00:23:58.364
This could still be an infection
or cancer.
Um-

360
00:23:58.432 --> 00:24:01.366
They don't fit any of
the most recent symptoms.

361
00:24:01.435 --> 00:24:04.232
Well, what, just hepatitis does?

362
00:24:04.305 --> 00:24:07.967
I know, I know, I know-
you can never be sure.

363
00:24:10.944 --> 00:24:13.935
When Wendy was in the hospital,

364
00:24:15.348 --> 00:24:18.214
a doctor told us
there was this aggressive
experimental treatment...

365
00:24:18.285 --> 00:24:21.685
which might extend her life
by two or three years.

366
00:24:22.755 --> 00:24:27.248
Well, we figured if there was any hope
at all that we could have her with us
a little while longer, it'd be worth it.

367
00:24:28.427 --> 00:24:31.418
Three weeks later, she was gone.

368
00:24:34.866 --> 00:24:36.856
I don't think it's hepatitis.

369
00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:41.530
I think your son has lupus.

370
00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:45.474
I think it's broken.

371
00:24:45.542 --> 00:24:47.532
What did you do?

372
00:24:48.545 --> 00:24:51.479
Accidentally
closed the car door on it.

373
00:24:51.548 --> 00:24:53.742
No.
Door would've broken the skin.

374
00:24:53.817 --> 00:24:58.412
This looks like something
hard and smooth smashed it.

375
00:24:58.487 --> 00:25:00.420
I want my lawyer.

376
00:25:00.489 --> 00:25:03.423
The brain has
a gating mechanism for pain.

377
00:25:03.492 --> 00:25:07.052
Registers the most severe injury
and blocks out the others.

378
00:25:07.128 --> 00:25:10.460
Did it work?
Well, my hand hurts like hell.

379
00:25:12.133 --> 00:25:15.067
Yeah. I feel much better.
Huh.

380
00:25:16.137 --> 00:25:18.070
Don't splint it.

381
00:25:18.139 --> 00:25:22.575
I want to be able to
bang it against the wall if I need to
administer another dose.

382
00:25:22.643 --> 00:25:25.338
Just tape it up.

383
00:25:29.583 --> 00:25:32.016
Why did you tell Cameron
to lie to Mr. Foster?

384
00:25:32.085 --> 00:25:34.245
Make it tight.
Answer me.

385
00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:36.253
[Sighs]

386
00:25:36.322 --> 00:25:39.290
Nothing I can say
is gonna change how you feel,

387
00:25:39.359 --> 00:25:42.293
and nothing can come out of
your reaction that is gonna change
what I plan to do,

388
00:25:42.361 --> 00:25:44.294
so I prefer to say nothing.

389
00:25:44.363 --> 00:25:46.796
So that was just you
saying nothing.

390
00:25:46.865 --> 00:25:48.798
Uh-huh.

391
00:25:48.867 --> 00:25:51.926
- The guy is furious.
- And scared.

392
00:25:52.004 --> 00:25:54.938
So what are you gonna do?
The father's insisting
on the lupus treatment.

393
00:25:55.006 --> 00:25:58.372
Yeah, Cameron told me,
and I told her to tell him no.

394
00:25:58.442 --> 00:26:01.376
Well, you can't just sit back
and let the kid die.

395
00:26:01.445 --> 00:26:03.605
Neither can the father.
So that's your plan?

396
00:26:03.681 --> 00:26:05.705
You're gonna play chicken
with the kid's life?

397
00:26:05.783 --> 00:26:08.615
Well, he's the dad.
I should win easily.

398
00:26:08.685 --> 00:26:11.050
Take the week off.

399
00:26:11.121 --> 00:26:13.554
Why, 'cause I lied to a patient?

400
00:26:13.623 --> 00:26:16.056
I take risks.
Sometimes patients die.

401
00:26:16.126 --> 00:26:19.060
But not taking risks
causes more patients to die,

402
00:26:19.128 --> 00:26:22.324
so I guess my biggest problem is
I've been cursed
with the ability to do the math.

403
00:26:24.433 --> 00:26:28.493
[Sighs]
I told him that we wouldn't
treat him for the lupus until-

404
00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:32.164
- What'd he say?
- He said he wanted to transfer Keith
to another hospital.

405
00:26:32.240 --> 00:26:35.208
He's not stable enough.
He'd never make it through the door.
That's what I told him.

406
00:26:35.276 --> 00:26:37.903
And that's when he caved.

407
00:26:37.979 --> 00:26:41.141
Yeah.
He agreed to do it your way.

408
00:26:42.215 --> 00:26:43.977
Two plus two equals four.

409
00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:53.384
If it is hepatitis "E,"
we should see a marked decline
in liver function tests within the hour.

410
00:26:53.459 --> 00:26:56.393
Why bother explaining it to me?
It's not like I have any choice
in the matter.

411
00:26:56.462 --> 00:26:59.157
If there's no hep "E,"
we'll start treatment for lupus immediately.

412
00:26:59.231 --> 00:27:01.164
Ouch.

413
00:27:01.233 --> 00:27:03.223
- Keith? What's wrong?
- What's happening?

414
00:27:03.302 --> 00:27:05.235
No, get off.

415
00:27:05.303 --> 00:27:08.760
Keith, it's Dr. Chase.
Where does it hurt?
Jules, no!

416
00:27:08.840 --> 00:27:10.932
- He's hallucinating.
- Is this from the medicine?

417
00:27:11.009 --> 00:27:14.500
- We haven't started the medicine.
- Keith, you're in the hospital.
Keith, there's nothing on you.

418
00:27:14.579 --> 00:27:17.945
- Hey. Hey. Keith.
- Keith! Keith! Keith!

419
00:27:18.015 --> 00:27:20.539
Hey. You okay, buddy?

420
00:27:20.617 --> 00:27:23.346
[Panting]

421
00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:29.014
Um, I think I wet the bed.

422
00:27:29.092 --> 00:27:33.084
Oh-
Don't worry about it.
It's fine. Let's get you up.

423
00:27:35.465 --> 00:27:37.728
- [Foster] Oh, God.!
- He's had a major bleed.

424
00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:41.565
- Bright red blood per rectum.
- I didn't- I didn't mean to. I'm sorry.

425
00:27:41.636 --> 00:27:43.603
[Monitor Alarm Beeping]
He's going into
hypovolemic shock.

426
00:27:43.672 --> 00:27:47.732
- Pressure's 60, heart rate's 140.
- We need an angiography, stat!

427
00:27:53.114 --> 00:27:55.604
[Monitor Beeping]

428
00:27:57.685 --> 00:28:00.619
Angiography revealed
major upper and lower G.I. Bleeding,

429
00:28:00.688 --> 00:28:03.451
severe hemodynamic
compromise and liver failure.

430
00:28:03.524 --> 00:28:07.015
He's also hallucinating.
Thinks he's being tortured by
someone named Jules.

431
00:28:07.093 --> 00:28:09.026
Hallucinations
are a symptom of psychosis,

432
00:28:09.095 --> 00:28:11.528
which is the fourth
diagnostic criterion.

433
00:28:11.598 --> 00:28:13.531
It's official. This is lupus.

434
00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.534
Who's Jules?

435
00:28:16.602 --> 00:28:18.933
Any mention of her
in the medical history?

436
00:28:19.004 --> 00:28:22.461
It doesn't matter
what he's hallucinating about,
it matters why.! It's lupus.

437
00:28:22.541 --> 00:28:24.474
Well, there's no need
to get snippy.

438
00:28:24.543 --> 00:28:27.102
This kind oflupus takes years
to get to this point.

439
00:28:27.179 --> 00:28:28.577
It's been a week.
Yeah.

440
00:28:28.646 --> 00:28:32.775
And a 16-year-old kid
shouldn't have hemolytic anemia
or be bleeding out of every orifice, but he is.

441
00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:37.786
We had an opportunity to treat this.
Instead, we diddled around
with hepatitis "E,"and now it's too late.

442
00:28:37.855 --> 00:28:40.618
He needs a new liver.
We screwed up.

443
00:28:41.691 --> 00:28:43.590
You're saying I screwed up.

444
00:28:45.395 --> 00:28:47.760
- Yes.
- Then why don't you just say that?

445
00:28:49.966 --> 00:28:52.957
- You gonna blame this on her?
- Did you agree with my recommendation
to treat for hep "E"?

446
00:28:53.035 --> 00:28:56.265
- No, I didn't.
- And she made herself quite clear.

447
00:28:56.338 --> 00:29:00.103
- And then she went and lied to the father.
That's why you're angry.
- Yeah. I trusted you.

448
00:29:00.176 --> 00:29:03.736
You always trust me.
That's a big mistake.

449
00:29:03.812 --> 00:29:05.836
Lupus is a bad diagnosis.

450
00:29:05.914 --> 00:29:08.507
- It's the best diagnosis we've got.
- That doesn't make it good.

451
00:29:08.583 --> 00:29:12.018
No. Itjust makes it
this kid's only chance to live.

452
00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:19.083
Put him on the transplant list.

453
00:29:19.159 --> 00:29:21.353
And make sure Cuddy knows.

454
00:29:21.428 --> 00:29:24.419
See if she can do anything
to get him close to the top.

455
00:29:37.276 --> 00:29:39.266
[Vomiting]

456
00:29:43.148 --> 00:29:46.275
[Panting]

457
00:29:48.119 --> 00:29:50.052
Cafeteria.

458
00:29:50.121 --> 00:29:52.054
Stay away from the sushi.

459
00:29:52.122 --> 00:29:54.146
And what happened to your hand?

460
00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:58.484
Got stuck in a drawer.

461
00:29:58.562 --> 00:30:01.052
Yeah, right.
You're going through withdrawal.

462
00:30:01.131 --> 00:30:04.827
No, I am going through pain.

463
00:30:04.901 --> 00:30:07.835
Pain causes nausea.
[Sighs]

464
00:30:07.904 --> 00:30:11.999
I took this job to work with you,
not cover your ass.

465
00:30:12.942 --> 00:30:14.966
Your Vicodin.

466
00:30:17.412 --> 00:30:20.642
And your solution
is to give me drugs.

467
00:30:20.716 --> 00:30:22.649
That's interesting.
No.

468
00:30:22.718 --> 00:30:26.050
Now I'm covering my ass.

469
00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:28.747
Take your pills
before you kill this kid.

470
00:30:35.396 --> 00:30:37.386
[Door Opens]

471
00:30:39.332 --> 00:30:41.322
[Door Closes]

472
00:30:53.379 --> 00:30:55.369
[Sighs]

473
00:31:08.593 --> 00:31:11.026
Lupus is normally treated
with medication.

474
00:31:11.095 --> 00:31:13.756
But in Keith's case,
the disease is too advanced.

475
00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:18.789
Because you lied.
Because House wanted to
play games with my son's life.

476
00:31:18.869 --> 00:31:22.065
There's no way to really tell what
progression the disease may have taken-
You're right.

477
00:31:24.141 --> 00:31:26.734
And I'm sorry.

478
00:31:26.809 --> 00:31:28.799
[Inhales]

479
00:31:28.878 --> 00:31:30.640
So what do we do?

480
00:31:30.713 --> 00:31:32.646
He needs a new liver.

481
00:31:33.716 --> 00:31:37.151
There are over 15,000 patients
on the transplant list.

482
00:31:37.218 --> 00:31:40.084
But how many
are about to bleed to death
unless they get a new liver?

483
00:31:40.155 --> 00:31:42.622
In Jersey? I'd say, uh, 20.

484
00:31:43.758 --> 00:31:47.284
Two thousand patients die each year
because a donor liver can't be found.
That's almost five a day.

485
00:31:47.362 --> 00:31:49.056
So he's screwed.

486
00:31:51.031 --> 00:31:52.895
I'll see what I can do.

487
00:31:54.702 --> 00:31:58.296
- Could I donate part of my liver?
- Sorry. You're a different blood type.

488
00:31:58.372 --> 00:32:00.498
So we just wait?
I'm afraid so.

489
00:32:00.574 --> 00:32:02.837
And hope for someone to die.

490
00:32:05.011 --> 00:32:07.001
Who's Jules?

491
00:32:08.881 --> 00:32:11.576
Dr. House, you should
get back to your office-
Jules.

492
00:32:11.651 --> 00:32:15.017
- There's noJules in the history.
- It was a hallucination.

493
00:32:15.087 --> 00:32:17.020
Of what?
Our cat.

494
00:32:17.089 --> 00:32:19.522
Does this matter?
[Foreman]
No. I'm sorry.

495
00:32:19.591 --> 00:32:23.617
We'll continue the transfusions and
the treatment for the anemia and liver failure
while we're waiting for a donor.

496
00:32:23.695 --> 00:32:26.959
- How long can he wait?
- Not long.

497
00:32:27.031 --> 00:32:29.260
- I don't think this is lupus.
- It's lupus. Come on, let's just go-

498
00:32:29.333 --> 00:32:32.267
Your fourth diagnostic criterion
for lupus is psychosis.

499
00:32:32.336 --> 00:32:34.667
This is just a kid
missing his cat.

500
00:32:34.739 --> 00:32:38.003
He was being attacked by an animal
that wasn't in the room.

501
00:32:38.074 --> 00:32:41.270
That's psychosis.
There's a difference between
psychosis and hallucination.

502
00:32:41.344 --> 00:32:44.779
So if he was imagining a fake cat,
it'd be lupus, but since it
was a real cat, it's not?

503
00:32:44.848 --> 00:32:46.872
- Take your damn pills.
- Psychosis requires-

504
00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:49.008
There is no cat.

505
00:32:50.052 --> 00:32:52.315
Jules is dead.

506
00:32:54.523 --> 00:32:58.049
You have a dead family pet
and you never mentioned it?

507
00:32:58.127 --> 00:33:00.117
Nice family history.

508
00:33:00.194 --> 00:33:04.323
Family history's
asking about family members,
meaning people related to the patient.

509
00:33:04.399 --> 00:33:07.162
How did the cat die?
Can you get him outta here?

510
00:33:07.235 --> 00:33:10.795
- Dr. House, come on, let's-
- What happened to the cat?

511
00:33:12.005 --> 00:33:14.029
Old age.

512
00:33:14.107 --> 00:33:16.006
- She was 15 years old.
- When?

513
00:33:16.076 --> 00:33:17.804
About a month ago.

514
00:33:17.878 --> 00:33:20.175
- Does this have anything to do with-
- Where'd she sleep?

515
00:33:20.247 --> 00:33:22.077
With Keith.
This is not a cat allergy.

516
00:33:22.149 --> 00:33:24.013
And it's not lupus.

517
00:33:24.083 --> 00:33:26.050
Where is Jules?

518
00:33:29.221 --> 00:33:31.552
[Sighs]

519
00:33:31.624 --> 00:33:33.557
Four years of college,

520
00:33:33.626 --> 00:33:35.559
four at med school,

521
00:33:35.627 --> 00:33:37.560
two years of residency,

522
00:33:37.629 --> 00:33:40.563
another four
of subspecialty training,
and where do I end up?

523
00:33:42.267 --> 00:33:44.200
Talking instead of digging.

524
00:33:44.269 --> 00:33:46.429
Come on.
The ground's frozen solid.

525
00:33:54.111 --> 00:33:56.704
[Thumping]
[Sighs]

526
00:33:56.780 --> 00:33:58.610
[Foreman]
What's that?

527
00:34:47.126 --> 00:34:49.059
[Elevator Bell Dings]

528
00:34:49.128 --> 00:34:51.061
Out of the way!

529
00:34:52.064 --> 00:34:54.964
We have the liver.
Prep O.R. Four.

530
00:35:17.420 --> 00:35:19.785
[Monitors Beeping]

531
00:35:21.024 --> 00:35:24.652
All right, Keith,
start counting backward from ten.

532
00:35:24.727 --> 00:35:26.717
Ten,

533
00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:29.663
nine,

534
00:35:29.732 --> 00:35:32.359
eight-

535
00:35:32.434 --> 00:35:34.731
Scalpel.

536
00:35:40.008 --> 00:35:42.271
- [Door Slides Open]
- Stop the gases.

537
00:35:42.343 --> 00:35:43.809
What the hell are you doing,
House?

538
00:35:43.878 --> 00:35:49.144
Saving a 16-year-old kid from a lifetime
of immunosuppressant drugs
and a very nasty scar.

539
00:35:49.216 --> 00:35:51.809
This kid doesn't have
lupoid hepatitis.

540
00:35:51.885 --> 00:35:53.875
He has acute naphthalene toxicity.

541
00:35:53.954 --> 00:35:57.150
Naphthalene?
Y-Y-You're talking about mothballs.

542
00:35:57.223 --> 00:35:58.951
Nope. Termites.

543
00:36:00.226 --> 00:36:02.159
They create naphthalene
to protect their nests,

544
00:36:02.228 --> 00:36:07.358
which I'm assuming is rather large
and inside all four walls of his bedroom at home.

545
00:36:08.967 --> 00:36:10.900
And your assumption
is based on what?

546
00:36:10.969 --> 00:36:14.165
The autopsy I just conducted
on his pet cat.

547
00:36:14.239 --> 00:36:18.004
- Call Cuddy. And Security.
- You are not removing that kid's liver.

548
00:36:18.076 --> 00:36:19.838
Now!

549
00:36:20.911 --> 00:36:23.003
[Hawks, Spits]

550
00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:26.674
[Yells]
Have you completely lost your mind?

551
00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:29.479
No, but I have been feeling
a little sick lately.

552
00:36:29.553 --> 00:36:32.043
Ah-choo!
[Sighs]

553
00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:37.254
There's no way
we can do this surgery now.
Ya think?

554
00:36:37.327 --> 00:36:40.193
You've already cost him his liver.
Don't kill him too.

555
00:36:40.263 --> 00:36:43.197
Why are you so eager
to cut into a healthy kid?

556
00:36:43.266 --> 00:36:46.200
Healthy? He's in the toilet.
He just needs
some chicken soup.

557
00:36:46.268 --> 00:36:50.226
- I'm telling Hourani to re-scrub.
We're doing this transplant.
- No, you're not.

558
00:36:50.305 --> 00:36:54.673
You said it. If Keith's symptoms had
an environmental cause, they would have
disappeared as soon as he got here.

559
00:36:54.743 --> 00:36:56.232
They've only gotten worse.

560
00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:59.836
If the food here wasn't
one step below Riker's Island,
he would've gotten better.

561
00:36:59.914 --> 00:37:02.541
- He's lost 14 pounds-
- Sure. This is nothing but a dietary thing.

562
00:37:02.617 --> 00:37:05.244
Naphthalene is a gas,
a fat-soluble gas.

563
00:37:05.319 --> 00:37:08.253
Kid breathes it in,
it gets stored in his fat cells.

564
00:37:08.321 --> 00:37:11.619
Outside the hospital, his body
burned protein and carbs for energy.

565
00:37:11.691 --> 00:37:13.453
And the naphthalene
stayed in fat.

566
00:37:13.526 --> 00:37:18.292
But once the car accident
put him in the hospital
and he started losing weight,

567
00:37:18.365 --> 00:37:20.662
his body had to get its energy
somewhere else.

568
00:37:20.733 --> 00:37:23.326
It started to burn fat.

569
00:37:23.402 --> 00:37:24.333
The floodgates opened.

570
00:37:24.403 --> 00:37:27.462
The poison poured into his system.

571
00:37:27.539 --> 00:37:29.972
So getting away from the poison
is what poisoned him?

572
00:37:30.042 --> 00:37:32.976
Getting away from his dad's meat loaf
is what's killing him.

573
00:37:33.044 --> 00:37:35.705
You wanna explain to me
why you stopped the surgery?

574
00:37:35.780 --> 00:37:37.770
Oh, my God!
My God.

575
00:37:39.784 --> 00:37:42.274
- I want him locked up!
- [Chase] Hey.!

576
00:37:42.353 --> 00:37:44.445
Take it easy.

577
00:37:55.165 --> 00:37:58.156
Your cat did not die of old age.

578
00:38:01.270 --> 00:38:04.761
It died of massive internal bleeding
and acute liver failure...

579
00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:06.932
caused by naphthalene poisoning.

580
00:38:08.209 --> 00:38:10.836
The exact same thing
your son has.

581
00:38:10.912 --> 00:38:14.506
You lie to me,
you mess up my son's surgery,
and now you expect me to trust you?

582
00:38:14.582 --> 00:38:18.017
Give me 24 hours.
We'll pump your son full of calories-

583
00:38:18.086 --> 00:38:21.179
- That liver is going to somebody right now.
- We're doing that surgery.

584
00:38:21.255 --> 00:38:25.122
You do the surgery,
you'll be killing a mother of four.

585
00:38:25.192 --> 00:38:26.954
Father of three.

586
00:38:27.027 --> 00:38:29.790
- I was guessing.
- Like you are now?

587
00:38:31.230 --> 00:38:35.427
Naphthalene poisoning
is the best explanation we have
for what's wrong with your son.

588
00:38:36.502 --> 00:38:39.834
It explains the internal bleeding,
the hemolytic anemia,
the liver failure.

589
00:38:39.906 --> 00:38:42.305
It also predicts what'll happen next.

590
00:38:42.375 --> 00:38:46.435
If you do the surgery,
he's gonna lay on that table
for 14 hours...

591
00:38:46.511 --> 00:38:50.276
while his body continues to burn fat
and release poison into his system.

592
00:38:50.349 --> 00:38:53.010
Either way, I did you a favor.

593
00:38:53.085 --> 00:38:56.110
He's awake now.
You've got a chance to say good-bye.

594
00:39:03.694 --> 00:39:05.684
I think you should trust Dr. House.

595
00:39:18.907 --> 00:39:21.238
Give the liver to the other guy.

596
00:39:35.757 --> 00:39:37.621
Watch out.

597
00:39:59.078 --> 00:40:02.444
I.N.R.'s down
and his red count is climbing.

598
00:40:04.917 --> 00:40:07.407
Means you made the right call.

599
00:40:07.485 --> 00:40:10.180
His liver is healing.
He's gonna be just fine.

600
00:40:10.255 --> 00:40:12.347
[Sobbing]

601
00:40:27.704 --> 00:40:29.637
You made it a week.

602
00:40:29.705 --> 00:40:31.638
And won my prize.

603
00:40:31.707 --> 00:40:33.640
Congratulations.
Cuddy's a sucker.

604
00:40:33.709 --> 00:40:36.142
I would've done it
for two weeks off.

605
00:40:36.212 --> 00:40:38.145
Yeah, it was a piece of cake.

606
00:40:38.214 --> 00:40:39.646
You learn anything?

607
00:40:40.982 --> 00:40:42.573
Yeah.

608
00:40:42.651 --> 00:40:44.641
I'm an addict.

609
00:40:54.295 --> 00:40:56.285
Uh-

610
00:40:57.465 --> 00:40:59.295
Okay.

611
00:40:59.366 --> 00:41:01.856
I'm not stopping.

612
00:41:04.371 --> 00:41:06.304
There are programs.

613
00:41:06.372 --> 00:41:08.305
Cuddy would give you the time.

614
00:41:08.374 --> 00:41:11.501
You could get on a different
pain management regimen.
I don't need to stop.

615
00:41:12.812 --> 00:41:14.779
You just said-

616
00:41:14.848 --> 00:41:18.908
I said I was an addict.
I didn't say I had a problem.

617
00:41:18.984 --> 00:41:21.076
I pay my bills. I make my meals.
I function.

618
00:41:22.921 --> 00:41:26.185
Is that all you want?
You have no relationships.

619
00:41:26.258 --> 00:41:28.623
I don't want any relationships.

620
00:41:28.693 --> 00:41:32.128
- You alienate people.
- I've been alienating people
since I was three.

621
00:41:32.196 --> 00:41:34.629
Oh, come on!
Drop it.

622
00:41:34.699 --> 00:41:37.166
You don't think you've changed
in the last few years?

623
00:41:37.235 --> 00:41:40.465
Well, of course I have.
I've- I've gotten older,
my hair's got thinner.

624
00:41:40.537 --> 00:41:44.438
Sometimes I'm bored.
Sometimes I'm lonely.
Sometimes I wonder what it all means.

625
00:41:44.508 --> 00:41:45.872
No, I was there.

626
00:41:45.942 --> 00:41:48.603
You are not just a regular guy
who's getting older.

627
00:41:48.678 --> 00:41:51.544
You've changed. You're miserable,
and you're afraid to face yourself.

628
00:41:51.615 --> 00:41:54.276
Of course I've changed!

629
00:42:01.323 --> 00:42:03.882
And everything's the leg?

630
00:42:03.958 --> 00:42:06.949
Nothing's the pills?
They haven't done a thing to you?

631
00:42:08.596 --> 00:42:11.462
They let me do my job.

632
00:42:11.533 --> 00:42:14.160
And they take away my pain.

633
00:42:29.682 --> 00:42:31.672
[Door Closes]

634
00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:48.063
How'd it go?

635
00:42:48.133 --> 00:42:50.566
He admitted
he's addicted to the narcotics.

636
00:42:50.635 --> 00:42:54.126
Well, admitting you
have a problem is the first-
And he says it's not a problem.

637
00:42:55.472 --> 00:42:57.405
Maybe it's not.

638
00:42:57.474 --> 00:42:59.407
What do I know?

639
00:42:59.476 --> 00:43:02.740
What are you gonna do?
Nothing.
Done enough damage.

640
00:43:02.813 --> 00:43:06.748
Better hope he never finds out
that that was your idea.
He'd never believe it.

641
00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:02.261
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0.500 --> 00:00:2.00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>

